            <div xml:id="d701">

                <pb facs="0673" n="669" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg669"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51 Salt Funds/170: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-03-12T10:00:00+08:00">March 12, 1938—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 12—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>154. Shanghai’s 371, March 9, 4 p.m. It is my opinion that in dealing with the
                    situation which faces the Salt Administration we should remain as aloof in the
                    matter of offering advice to the Chinese as we have been in regard to the
                    Customs Administration. Chinese Government is unable to do anything for
                    administration of this tax in areas occupied by the Japanese. Foreign
                    governments are in no position to substitute themselves individually or in
                    combination for Chinese control. Japanese are prepared and in fact desire to
                    substitute Japanese control for Chinese control of these taxes. Under the
                    circumstances it seems to me that we must in the end hold Japanese responsible
                    for any claim which our nationals have upon the revenues in question.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping, Shanghai. Shanghai please repeat to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
